exec(open(__file__.rsplit("/",1)[0] + "/_h.py").read())
B("1 Samuel", """
1|Hannah, who has no children, prays at Shiloh for a son. God gives her Samuel, and she gives him back to serve God at the tent.
2|Hannah sings a prayer of thanks. Eli's sons Hophni and Phinehas abuse their office as priests, while the boy Samuel grows in the Lord's service. A prophet warns Eli.
3|God calls Samuel by name in the night. Samuel tells Eli the Lord's message of judgment on his house, and all Israel knows he is a prophet.
4|The Philistines defeat Israel and capture the ark of the covenant. Eli's sons are killed, and Eli dies when he hears the news.
5|The Philistines put the ark in the temple of Dagon. The statue of Dagon falls, and a plague breaks out wherever the ark is taken.
6|The Philistines send the ark back to Israel on a cart with offerings. It arrives at Beth Shemesh.
7|Samuel calls Israel to turn from idols. The Lord thunders against the Philistines and Israel wins. Samuel sets up a stone called Ebenezer, which means: Thus far the Lord has helped us.
8|The people ask Samuel for a king. God tells Samuel they have rejected him, and Samuel warns them what a king will cost. They insist.
9|Saul, a tall young man from Benjamin, is looking for lost donkeys and meets Samuel. God tells Samuel that Saul is the one to lead Israel.
10|Samuel anoints Saul privately and gives him signs. At Mizpah Saul is chosen by lot as king, though he is hiding in the baggage.
11|Ammon threatens Jabesh Gilead. The Spirit comes on Saul and he rallies Israel and rescues the city. Saul is confirmed as king.
12|Samuel gives his farewell speech. He reminds Israel to fear the Lord and serve him, and says he will keep praying for them.
13|Saul offers a sacrifice himself, instead of waiting for Samuel. Samuel tells him that his kingdom will not last.
14|Jonathan and his armor-bearer attack a Philistine outpost, and God sends panic. Saul makes a foolish oath, and the people save Jonathan from being put to death.
15|God tells Saul to destroy Amalek. Saul spares the king and the best animals. Samuel tells him: to obey is better than sacrifice, and God has rejected him as king.
16|God sends Samuel to Bethlehem to anoint one of Jesse's sons. God says: the Lord looks at the heart. Samuel anoints David. David plays his harp for Saul.
17|The Philistine champion Goliath challenges Israel for forty days. David, a shepherd boy, kills him with a sling and a stone, trusting in the Lord.
18|Jonathan and David become close friends. Saul grows jealous of David's success and tries to kill him, and then gives him his daughter Michal as a wife.
19|Saul orders David killed. Jonathan and Michal help David escape. Saul and his men are overcome by the Spirit when they go to Samuel.
20|Jonathan tests Saul's mind and learns that his father wants David dead. Jonathan and David say goodbye and renew their covenant.
21|David flees to the priest Ahimelech at Nob, who gives him holy bread and Goliath's sword. David pretends to be insane before the king of Gath.
22|David hides in the cave of Adullam, and four hundred men join him. Saul has the priests of Nob killed, and only Abiathar escapes.
23|David saves the town of Keilah. Jonathan visits David and encourages him. Saul pursues David in the wilderness.
24|David has a chance to kill Saul in a cave and instead cuts off a corner of his robe. He tells Saul he will not harm the Lord's anointed, and Saul admits David will be king.
25|Samuel dies. A rich man, Nabal, insults David, but Nabal's wife Abigail brings gifts and stops David from taking revenge. Nabal dies, and David marries Abigail.
26|David again spares Saul's life, taking his spear and water jug while Saul sleeps.
27|David lives among the Philistines, in Ziklag, and tells King Achish he is raiding Israel. He is really raiding Israel's enemies.
28|Saul, afraid before a Philistine attack, visits a medium at Endor. Samuel's spirit says Saul and his sons will die.
29|The Philistine commanders will not let David fight with them, so Achish sends him home.
30|Amalekites have burned Ziklag and taken the families. David pursues and rescues everyone, and shares the plunder with those who stayed behind.
31|Israel loses at Mount Gilboa. Saul's sons, including Jonathan, are killed, and Saul falls on his own sword.
""")
B("2 Samuel", """
1|David hears that Saul and Jonathan are dead. He has the messenger who claims to have killed Saul put to death, and he sings a lament for the two.
2|David is anointed king of Judah at Hebron. Abner makes Saul's son Ish-bosheth king of the rest of Israel. A war breaks out between the two houses.
3|The war continues, and David grows stronger. Abner offers to bring Israel to David, but Joab kills Abner. David mourns him publicly.
4|Ish-bosheth is murdered by two of his officers. David has them executed for killing an innocent man.
5|All Israel makes David king. He takes Jerusalem and makes it his capital, and defeats the Philistines.
6|David brings the ark to Jerusalem. A man named Uzzah dies when he touches it. David dances before the Lord when it arrives, and Michal despises him.
7|David wants to build a house for God. God says David's son will build it, and promises that David's throne will last forever.
8|David wins victories over the Philistines, Moab, Aram, Edom and others, and Israel is secure. A list of his officials is given.
9|David asks if anyone is left from Saul's family and shows kindness to Jonathan's disabled son, Mephibosheth, giving him a place at the king's table.
10|David sends messengers to the king of Ammon, who humiliates them. Israel defeats Ammon and the Arameans.
11|David stays home during war, sees Bathsheba bathing, and takes her. When she is pregnant, he has her husband Uriah killed in battle. God is displeased.
12|The prophet Nathan tells David a story that exposes his sin. David confesses, and God forgives him, but the child dies. Bathsheba later bears Solomon.
13|David's son Amnon rapes his half-sister Tamar. Her brother Absalom kills Amnon two years later and flees.
14|Joab arranges for Absalom to return to Jerusalem. Absalom finally sees the king, who forgives him.
15|Absalom wins the people's hearts and starts a rebellion. David flees Jerusalem, weeping, with his loyal followers.
16|David is cursed by Shimei. Absalom enters Jerusalem and takes advice from Ahithophel.
17|Hushai gives false advice that saves David, and Ahithophel kills himself. David crosses the Jordan and is supported by friends.
18|David's army defeats Absalom's. Joab kills Absalom as he hangs in a tree, and David weeps: O my son Absalom.
19|Joab tells David to stop mourning and encourage the army. David returns to Jerusalem and Israel and Judah argue about who will bring the king back.
20|Sheba rebels against David. Joab kills Amasa and pursues Sheba, whose head is thrown over a city wall. David's officials are listed.
21|A three-year famine comes because Saul broke a treaty with the Gibeonites. David hands over seven of Saul's sons and then buries the bones of Saul and Jonathan. Israel fights the Philistines.
22|David sings a song of praise to God, who is his rock, fortress and deliverer, after being rescued from all his enemies.
23|These are David's last words. His mighty men are named, including the three who broke through enemy lines to bring him water.
24|David counts the people, and God sends a plague as punishment. David buys the threshing floor of Araunah and builds an altar there, the site of the future temple.
""")
B("1 Kings", """
1|David is old, and his son Adonijah tries to make himself king. Nathan and Bathsheba get David to name Solomon king, and Solomon is anointed.
2|David tells Solomon to follow God's law and to deal with Joab and Shimei. David dies after forty years as king. Solomon puts Adonijah, Joab and Shimei to death.
3|Solomon marries Pharaoh's daughter. At Gibeon God offers him anything, and he asks for wisdom. God also gives him riches and honor. He judges between two women who claim the same baby.
4|Solomon's officials and officers are listed. His kingdom is at peace, and his wisdom is greater than anyone's.
5|Solomon makes a treaty with King Hiram of Tyre, who sends cedar and workers for the temple. Solomon organizes forced labor.
6|Solomon begins to build the temple in Jerusalem in the fourth year of his reign. The chapter describes its size, its cedar and gold, and the cherubim in the inner room.
7|Solomon builds his palace and other buildings. Hiram of Tyre makes the bronze pillars, the large basin called the Sea, and other temple furnishings.
8|Solomon brings the ark into the temple, and the glory of the Lord fills it. He prays a long prayer of dedication and blesses the people.
9|God appears to Solomon a second time and promises to bless him if he obeys, and warns him if he does not. Solomon builds cities and a fleet.
10|The queen of Sheba visits Solomon and is amazed by his wisdom and wealth. Solomon's gold and trade are described.
11|Solomon marries many foreign women, and they turn his heart to other gods. God tells him the kingdom will be torn away from his son. Solomon dies and his son Rehoboam becomes king.
12|Rehoboam rejects the elders' advice and threatens the people with harsher rule. Ten tribes break away under Jeroboam, and the kingdom splits. Jeroboam sets up golden calves at Bethel and Dan.
13|A man of God from Judah prophesies against Jeroboam's altar at Bethel. He disobeys God's command about eating, and is killed by a lion.
14|Jeroboam's son is sick, and the prophet Ahijah announces judgment on his house. Rehoboam reigns in Judah, and Shishak of Egypt invades.
15|Abijam and then Asa reign in Judah, and Asa does what is right. Nadab and then Baasha reign in Israel.
16|Israel has several short-reign kings: Elah, Zimri, and then Omri, who builds Samaria. Omri's son Ahab marries Jezebel and worships Baal.
17|Elijah tells Ahab that there will be no rain. Ravens feed him, and then a widow at Zarephath shares her last food. Elijah raises her son from the dead.
18|After three years of drought, Elijah challenges the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel. Fire from heaven burns Elijah's offering, and the rain returns.
19|Jezebel threatens Elijah, and he runs away and despairs. God speaks to him in a gentle whisper at Mount Horeb, and tells him to anoint Elisha as his successor.
20|Ben-Hadad of Aram attacks Samaria. A prophet tells Ahab that God will give Israel victory, and Israel wins twice. Ahab makes a treaty with Ben-Hadad that God did not allow.
21|Ahab wants Naboth's vineyard. Jezebel has Naboth killed, and Ahab takes it. Elijah tells Ahab that God will judge his house, and Ahab repents for a time.
22|Ahab and Jehoshaphat go to war with Aram. The prophet Micaiah warns that Ahab will die, and Ahab is killed in battle.
""")
B("2 Kings", """
1|Ahaziah of Israel is injured and sends to ask the god Baal-zebub if he will recover. Elijah tells him he will die. Fire from heaven consumes two groups of soldiers.
2|Elijah is taken up to heaven in a whirlwind with chariots of fire. Elisha receives a double share of his spirit, and the prophets of Jericho recognize it.
3|Israel, Judah and Edom go to war with Moab. Elisha promises water and victory. The king of Moab sacrifices his son on the wall.
4|Elisha multiplies a widow's oil. He promises a son to a woman in Shunem and later raises that boy from the dead. He purifies poisoned stew and feeds a hundred men with twenty loaves.
5|Naaman, an Aramean commander with a skin disease, is told by Elisha to wash seven times in the Jordan. He is healed. Elisha's servant Gehazi gets greedy and is struck with the disease.
6|An axe head floats. Elisha traps the Aramean army, but then feeds them and sends them home. A siege brings famine to Samaria.
7|Four men with leprosy find the Aramean camp empty because God made the Arameans flee. The famine ends, as Elisha said.
8|The Shunammite woman gets her land back. Elisha weeps as he tells Hazael he will be king of Aram. Jehoram and Ahaziah reign in Judah.
9|Elisha sends a prophet to anoint Jehu as king of Israel. Jehu kills Joram, Ahaziah of Judah, and Jezebel, as Elijah had said.
10|Jehu kills the rest of Ahab's family and the worshippers of Baal. He does not turn from Jeroboam's golden calves.
11|Athaliah kills the royal family and rules Judah. The priest Jehoiada hides the baby Joash for six years, then crowns him and has Athaliah killed.
12|Joash repairs the temple under Jehoiada's guidance. Later he pays tribute to Hazael of Aram, and his own officials kill him.
13|Jehoahaz and Jehoash reign in Israel. Elisha dies after telling Jehoash to strike the ground with arrows. A dead man touches Elisha's bones and comes to life.
14|Amaziah of Judah defeats Edom, but is defeated by Israel. Jeroboam II reigns in Israel, and Jonah's prophecy is fulfilled.
15|Several kings reign in Judah and Israel. Many of them are killed in coups, and Assyria begins to take Israel's land.
16|Ahaz of Judah worships idols and copies a pagan altar. He asks Assyria for help against Israel and Aram.
17|Assyria conquers Israel and carries its people away in 722 BC, because they worshipped other gods. Foreigners are settled in the land, and the Samaritans come from them.
18|Hezekiah of Judah tears down the high places and trusts the Lord. Assyria's king Sennacherib attacks and his commander mocks Hezekiah and God.
19|Hezekiah prays, and Isaiah tells him that Jerusalem will be saved. An angel of the Lord kills 185,000 Assyrians in one night.
20|Hezekiah is sick, and God adds fifteen years to his life. He shows his treasures to Babylonian visitors, and Isaiah warns that Babylon will one day take them.
21|Manasseh reigns fifty-five years, rebuilds the high places and worships idols. His son Amon is killed after two years.
22|Josiah becomes king at eight. During temple repairs the book of the law is found, and he is moved to repent. The prophet Huldah says disaster will come, but not in his lifetime.
23|Josiah reads the law to the people, renews the covenant and removes idols. He celebrates Passover. He is killed by Pharaoh Neco at Megiddo, and his sons are made kings and removed.
24|Babylon's king Nebuchadnezzar attacks Jerusalem, and takes Jehoiachin and many people to Babylon. He sets up Zedekiah as king, who rebels.
25|Babylon destroys Jerusalem and the temple in 586 BC and takes the people into exile. Years later Jehoiachin is released from prison.
""")
